  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CDRI Infrastructure for Resilient Island States (IRIS) Phase 1</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">&#x2018;Infrastructure for Resilient Island States&#x2019; (IRIS) supports Small Island Developing States (SIDS) with quality technical assistance and increased access to resources to build resilience of infrastructure to climate and disaster risks.  The Coalition for Disaster Resilient Infrastructure (CDRI) initiated IRIS in 2021. IRIS will also provide technical assistance, research and knowledge products to support mainstreaming of gender action plans within infrastructure activities, and to strengthen local technical skills and capacity. IRIS will not invest in construction directly.
The CDRI global partnership was founded in 2019 and is led by India. CDRI has 43 member countries and 7 member organisations, including all Quad partners.
The Prime Ministers of India, Australia, Fiji, Jamaica, Mauritius and UK launched the CDRI-IRIS initiative in November 2021 at COP26.  At this launch, PM Morrison announced that Australia would contribute AUD 10 million to CDRI-IRIS. Funding runs across 2 phases.</narrative>
  </description>
